        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters across Florida",
        "market_text": "<p>Florida is a remote-buyer market: northern relocations, snowbirds and international purchasers routinely shortlist — and sometimes buy — from photos alone. When the buyer can't attend the open house, the listing photos are the open house, and an empty white-tile room doesn't sell a Florida lifestyle to someone scrolling from New Jersey in February.</p><p>The stock skews vacant, too — investor condos, estate sales and new spec builds — so whole-home staging is routine here. Virtually staging an entire 8-photo listing costs about what one month of physically staging a single room would.</p><p>Florida's selling calendar is also inverted relative to most of the country. The buying season peaks when northern buyers are escaping winter, which means listings need to look their best in months when much of the US market is quiet. Photos that are ready on demand matter more in a market whose busiest weeks arrive in January.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Staged for the Florida buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Our designers stage coastal-bright for Miami and Fort Lauderdale condos, airy transitional for Orlando and Tampa family homes, and elegant looks for Naples and Sarasota. Every image is MLS-ready in 24 to 48 hours, disclosed as virtually staged.</p><p>Outdoor space gets staged as living space here rather than as an afterthought — lanais, pool decks and screened porches are where Florida listings win the scroll, and they photograph empty worse than any interior room.</p>",
